Task: Define the indexed batch-response contract (Phase 1, FR-2)

Files modified:
- server/src/services/ReleaseParser.ts

Summary:
Added IndexedParsedReleaseSchema (ParsedReleaseWithScoreSchema plus a
nullable 1-based 'index'), rewired BatchResponseSchema to use it, exported
the IndexedParsedRelease and BatchParseSlot types, and rewrote the tail of
BATCH_PROMPT to require the index echo on every entry.

Why:
MediaSearchService.ts:580 zips parseBatch output onto releases by array
position. If the model drops title #3 of 8, results 4-8 shift up one slot
and five releases receive another release's parse. That parse's
relevanceScore is used directly as confidenceScore in
CustomFormatScoringEngine.scoreCandidateUnified(), and both RssMediaMonitor
and WantedSearchService auto-grab at totalScore >= 50 - so the failure mode
is an automatic download of the wrong release, not a cosmetic display bug.
Truncation was reproduced live on two separate models, so this is an
observed defect rather than a theoretical one.

Design notes:
- index is .nullable().catch(null), not required. A model that ignores the
  instruction must degrade to the existing strict-length positional path,
  not fail the whole batch. Fail-soft on the instruction, fail-closed on
  the attribution.
- BatchParseSlot documents the contract change explicitly: the returned
  array is always titles.length long and slots may be null. Callers must
  stop assuming a populated slot.
- parseFiles() already carries the length guard parseBatch lacks
  (ReleaseParser.ts:337). The Phase 3 implementation follows that
  in-file precedent rather than inventing a new shape.
- Prompt wording tells the model to emit an entry even for titles it cannot
  parse, so truncation becomes rarer as well as harmless.

Verification: npx tsc -p server/tsconfig.json --noEmit -> 0 diagnostics.
Contract only; parseBatch still zips positionally until Phase 3.
